# MASTER PROJECT PROMPT: ENERGYGUARD AI

## 1. PROJECT TITLE

EnergyGuard AI – IoT-Enabled Intelligent Energy Monitoring and Optimization System for Small-Scale Manufacturing Industries

## 2. PROJECT OBJECTIVE

Build a complete, professional, functional, full-stack web application named EnergyGuard AI.

The application must monitor, analyze, visualize, and optimize electrical energy consumption in small-scale manufacturing industries using IoT sensors, real-time data communication, machine-level energy monitoring, AI-based anomaly detection, predictive alerts, and energy-saving recommendations.

This is an academic engineering project based on the uploaded EnergyGuard AI poster and PowerPoint presentation.

The application must represent the following project objectives:

* Small manufacturing factories waste energy.
* Factory owners lack real-time energy consumption information.
* Machines may consume energy while idle.
* Electrical bills are unnecessarily high.
* Existing monitoring methods may detect problems only after machine failure.
* Factory owners need affordable, understandable, and actionable energy monitoring.
* The proposed solution uses low-cost IoT sensors, Wi-Fi, a live dashboard, and AI-assisted optimization.

The application must support the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals:

SDG 7: Affordable and Clean Energy.

SDG 9: Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure.

The primary users are small factory owners, supervisors, operators, energy managers, and maintenance personnel.

The application must be designed for practical demonstration, academic evaluation, future hardware integration, and possible real-world deployment.

IMPORTANT:

Build the actual complete project.

Do not stop after creating a frontend.

Do not create only a dashboard mockup.

Do not use static hardcoded readings as the only data source.

Implement the frontend, backend, database, APIs, IoT integration, analytics, AI functionality, authentication, testing, and setup documentation.

Clearly distinguish simulated data from real sensor data.

